<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <application>

        <!--
          Receives watch → phone Data Layer messages (Story 10-6).

          This is the whole reason the transport is a local Expo module rather than a file in
          `android/`: the manifest merger folds this <service> into the app manifest that
          `expo prebuild` generates, so the declaration survives the clean prebuild that would
          erase anything written into `android/` directly.

          (Note for future editors: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, so flags like
          the one on that prebuild command cannot be written out here. The manifest parses as XML
          before the merger ever sees it, and the resulting failure names only the file.)

          WHY A SERVICE AND NOT A LIVE `MessageClient.addListener`:
          the watch flushes its CARD_USED outbox whenever it regains connectivity, which is
          almost never a moment when the React Native app happens to be running. A live
          listener only exists while JS is alive, so those events would be dropped — and the
          watch has already deleted them from its outbox on a successful send. The service
          starts the process, persists the message to `WearDataLayerInbox`, and JS drains it
          whenever it next runs. Without this, AC9's "events are never lost" is false.

          Correspondingly there is NO live listener anywhere in this module: the service is the
          single inbound path, so an event cannot be delivered twice.

          `android:exported="true"` is required — the system cannot bind the service otherwise
          (Android's own Data Layer guide states this). `tools:ignore="ExportedService"`
          silences the lint warning that the export is unguarded: the intent filter is
          restricted to the `wear://` scheme, which only Google Play services can dispatch.

          The `pathPrefix` restricts delivery to this app's own namespace, so an unrelated
          Data Layer path on the same node never spins up our process.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".WearDataLayerListenerService"
            android:exported="true"
            tools:ignore="ExportedService">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED" />
                <data
                    android:scheme="wear"
                    android:host="*"
                    android:pathPrefix="/myloyaltycards" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
